rust-proxmox-daemon (1.1.0-1) trixie; urgency=medium

  * avoid a reload hanging forever when another thread of the daemon was
    spawning a process at that moment. The new server got stuck before it could
    start, while systemd already knew it as the main process of the service.

  * refuse connections on stop as soon as the service closed its listener,
    instead of queuing them until the process exits and then resetting them.

  * keep waiting for systemd to process the reload notifications if a signal
    interrupts that wait, so that the new server only goes on once systemd knows
    it as the new main process.

  * pass a stop that arrives during a reload on to the new server, which then
    shuts down through its regular stop path. Before, the new server could keep
    running as main process of a stopping service until the systemd stop timeout
    killed it. A stop that reaches the new server in its first moments, before
    it set up its signal handling, still ends it at once. Whichever comes first,
    a reload or a shutdown request, now decides for good.

  * handle SIGTERM like SIGINT as a request for a graceful shutdown. Daemons
    without their own SIGTERM handler, such as those of PBS and PDM, now stop
    accepting connections, let open requests finish and wait for running workers
    before they exit, up to the systemd stop timeout, instead of getting
    terminated at once.

 -- Proxmox Support Team <support@proxmox.com>  Fri, 13 Nov 2026 20:34:04 +0100

rust-proxmox-daemon (1.0.1-1) trixie; urgency=medium

  * reap the intermediate process of a reload before announcing the new main
    process to systemd. If it had not exited yet, systemd did not supervise the
    new process as its own child, could not collect its exit status, and for
    units with KillMode=mixed killed the service with SIGKILL on stop instead
    of sending SIGTERM first.

  * keep a shutdown or reload request made before the daemon starts waiting for
    one, for example a SIGHUP right after startup. Such a request got lost, and
    the daemon then no longer reacted to any further SIGINT or SIGHUP.

 -- Proxmox Support Team <support@proxmox.com>  Thu, 12 Nov 2026 23:58:28 +0100

